        qs: [
          { q: "Boston’s metro pairs fast salary growth with slow population growth. What does that usually point to?",
            opts: [["A supply constraint — people are being priced out",1],["Weak demand for workers",0],["A shrinking export base",0]],
            fb: "When pay is bid up but people cannot move in, the binding constraint is usually the cost and supply of housing." },
          { q: "Which signal would point at housing as the wall?",
            opts: [["Home values rising faster than the national trend",1],["Falling rents",0],["Slow wage growth",0]],
            fb: "Fast-rising home values alongside strong pay growth is what being priced out looks like in the data." }
        ],
        prompt: "After these charts, what do you now suspect is binding your city’s growth — and which chart made you think so?",
